Title:
PyPsPipeJack

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
PyPsPipeJack is a Python (Linux) tool that “pipe jacks” existing remote PowerShell (PSHost) named-pipe sessions over SMB, largely leveraging Impacket. If you have local admin on a Windows host, it lets you enumerate PSHost pipes and connect to one to run commands inside the already-running PowerShell session context (i.e., the logged-on user who owns that session). This can enable lateral movement and privilege escalation when higher-privileged users (e.g., Domain Admin) have an interactive PowerShell session open on the target. The tool supports multiple auth methods (NTLM hashes, password, Kerberos via ccache/AES key), can run single commands, run a full PS1 script, and has an interactive mode. The README also highlights using Impacket’s wmiquery to map a PSHost pipe’s PID to the owning user/session without executing commands in the hijacked session. It’s primarily useful for red teams/pentesters doing post-exploitation in AD environments and for defenders to understand/monitor this abuse path.

Technical Focus:
- SMB named pipes (IPC$) access and PSHost pipe enumeration
- Remote PowerShell session hijacking via existing PSHost processes
- Impacket-based authentication (NTLM, Kerberos ccache, AES keys)
- Command/script execution within another user’s PowerShell runspace
- WMI/WQL process-to-session/user correlation (Win32_Process, Win32_SessionProcess, Win32_LoggedOnUser)

Use Cases:
- Enumerate active PowerShell PSHost named pipes on a remote Windows system
- Execute commands as the security context of an existing privileged PowerShell session
- Post-exploitation privilege escalation when admins leave PowerShell sessions open
- Run PS1 automation through a hijacked session for rapid domain changes (e.g., group membership)
- Identify which user owns a PSHost pipe using WMI queries (without interacting with the session)

Keywords:
PyPsPipeJack, Impacket, SMB, IPC$, named pipe, PSHost, PowerShell runspace, session hijacking, lateral movement, privilege escalation, local administrator, NTLM, pass-the-hash, Kerberos, ccache, AES key, WMI, WQL, Win32_Process, Win32_LoggedOnUser